          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What is a migraine, and how is it different from an ordinary headache?",
          "answer": "A migraine is a recurring neurological condition. Headache is its most prominent feature, but it often comes with sensitivity to light and sound, and nausea. Most people have occasional attacks, and a small number have them very often. Source: \"Migraine\" (General clinical reference, educational summary).\n\nA typical migraine headache is usually one-sided and pulsating, moderate to severe, and worse with routine physical activity. Attacks often last several hours to three days if untreated. Not every attack has every feature, and the pain can be on both sides in many people. Source: \"Migraine\" (General clinical reference, educational summary).\n\nThe sources I searched do not give a full list of how to tell a migraine from other headache types. Some headaches need urgent attention. Sudden, severe headache that peaks within a minute, headache with fever and neck stiffness, headache with new neurological symptoms, or headache after a head injury need emergency assessment. If you have any of these, seek emergency care now. Sources: \"Sudden severe headache and other dangerous headache patterns\"; \"Migraine: acute treatment, when prevention starts, and medication-overuse limits\" (National headache society guidance, educational summary).\n\nIf you get recurring headaches, a clinician can help work out what type they are.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
